Custom Dashboard Creation with Grafana
Prerequisites
Basic Linux command-line knowledge
Understanding of system metrics (CPU, memory, disk usage)
Familiarity with web browsers and basic networking concepts
Completed Lab 1 or equivalent monitoring fundamentals
Learning Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Install and configure Grafana on a Linux system
Add and configure Prometheus as a data source
Create custom monitoring dashboards with multiple panels
Build queries to visualize system metrics
Export and share dashboard configurations
Environment Setup
System Requirements
Linux machine (Ubuntu 20.04+ or similar)
Minimum 2GB RAM
Internet connection for package downloads
Sudo privileges
Initial Setup
Update system packages:
sudo apt update
Install required dependencies:
sudo apt install -y wget curl software-properties-common
Task 1: Install and Configure Grafana
Step 1.1: Install Grafana
# Add Grafana GPG key
wget -q -O - https://packages.grafana.com/gpg.key | sudo apt-key add -

# Add Grafana repository
echo "deb https://packages.grafana.com/oss/deb stable main" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/grafana.list

# Update and install Grafana
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y grafana
Step 1.2: Start Grafana Service
# Enable Grafana to start on boot
sudo systemctl enable grafana-server

# Start Grafana service
sudo systemctl start grafana-server

# Verify Grafana is running
sudo systemctl status grafana-server
Expected Output: Service should show "active (running)" status.

Step 1.3: Access Grafana Web Interface
Open your web browser and navigate to:
http://localhost:3000
Default credentials:

Username: admin
Password: admin
Change the password when prompted (use a memorable password for this lab)

Step 1.4: Install Prometheus (Data Source)
# Create Prometheus user
sudo useradd --no-create-home --shell /bin/false prometheus

# Create directories
sudo mkdir /etc/prometheus
sudo mkdir /var/lib/prometheus

# Download Prometheus
cd /tmp
wget https://github.com/prometheus/prometheus/releases/download/v2.45.0/prometheus-2.45.0.linux-amd64.tar.gz

# Extract files
tar -xvf prometheus-2.45.0.linux-amd64.tar.gz
cd prometheus-2.45.0.linux-amd64

# Copy binaries
sudo cp prometheus /usr/local/bin/
sudo cp promtool /usr/local/bin/

# Copy configuration files
sudo cp -r consoles /etc/prometheus
sudo cp -r console_libraries /etc/prometheus
sudo cp prometheus.yml /etc/prometheus/
Step 1.5: Configure Prometheus
Create a basic Prometheus configuration:

sudo nano /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml
Add the following configuration (students should customize scrape_interval):

global:
  scrape_interval: # TODO: Set to 15s for this lab
  evaluation_interval: 15s

scrape_configs:
  - job_name: 'prometheus'
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['localhost:9090']
  
  - job_name: 'node_exporter'
    static_configs:
      - targets: # TODO: Add 'localhost:9100' as target
Step 1.6: Install Node Exporter
# Download Node Exporter
cd /tmp
wget https://github.com/prometheus/node_exporter/releases/download/v1.6.1/node_exporter-1.6.1.linux-amd64.tar.gz

# Extract and install
tar -xvf node_exporter-1.6.1.linux-amd64.tar.gz
sudo cp node_exporter-1.6.1.linux-amd64/node_exporter /usr/local/bin/
Step 1.7: Create Systemd Services
Create Node Exporter service:

sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/node_exporter.service
Add the following content:

[Unit]
Description=Node Exporter
After=network.target

[Service]
User=prometheus
Group=prometheus
Type=simple
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/node_exporter

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
Create Prometheus service:

sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/prometheus.service
Add the following content:

[Unit]
Description=Prometheus
After=network.target

[Service]
User=prometheus
Group=prometheus
Type=simple
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/prometheus \
  --config.file=/etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml \
  --storage.tsdb.path=/var/lib/prometheus/

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
Step 1.8: Set Permissions and Start Services
# Set ownership
sudo chown -R prometheus:prometheus /etc/prometheus
sudo chown -R prometheus:prometheus /var/lib/prometheus

# Reload systemd
sudo systemctl daemon-reload

# Start services
sudo systemctl start node_exporter
sudo systemctl start prometheus

# Enable services
sudo systemctl enable node_exporter
sudo systemctl enable prometheus

# Verify services
sudo systemctl status node_exporter
sudo systemctl status prometheus
Verification: Access Prometheus at http://localhost:9090 and verify targets are up under Status > Targets.

Task 2: Configure Data Sources and Create Dashboards
Step 2.1: Add Prometheus Data Source to Grafana
In Grafana web interface, click the gear icon (Configuration) in the left sidebar
Select Data Sources
Click Add data source
Select Prometheus
Configure the following:
Name: Prometheus
URL: http://localhost:9090
Leave other settings as default
Click Save & Test
Expected Result: Green message "Data source is working"

Step 2.2: Create Your First Dashboard
Click the + icon in the left sidebar
Select Dashboard
Click Add new panel
Step 2.3: Build CPU Usage Panel
Configure the panel with the following:

Query Section:

Data source: Prometheus
Metric: Enter the following PromQL query:
# TODO: Complete this query to show CPU usage
# Hint: Use rate() function with node_cpu_seconds_total metric
# Filter for mode!="idle" and calculate average
100 - (avg by(instance) (rate(node_cpu_seconds_total{mode="idle"}[5m])) * 100)
Panel Settings (right sidebar):

Title: CPU Usage (%)
Description: System CPU utilization percentage
Visualization:

Select Time series or Gauge (experiment with both)
Field Settings:

Unit: Percent (0-100)
Min: 0
Max: 100
Click Apply to save the panel

Step 2.4: Add Memory Usage Panel
Click Add panel at the top
Select Add new panel
Query:
# TODO: Calculate memory usage percentage
# Formula: (1 - (available_memory / total_memory)) * 100
# Use node_memory_MemAvailable_bytes and node_memory_MemTotal_bytes
(1 - (node_memory_MemAvailable_bytes / node_memory_MemTotal_bytes)) * 100
Panel Configuration:

Title: Memory Usage (%)
Visualization: Gauge
Unit: Percent (0-100)
Thresholds:
Green: 0-70
Yellow: 70-85
Red: 85-100
Click Apply

Step 2.5: Add Disk Usage Panel
Add another new panel
Query:
# TODO: Show available disk space
# Use node_filesystem_avail_bytes for root filesystem
# Filter: mountpoint="/"
100 - ((node_filesystem_avail_bytes{mountpoint="/"} / node_filesystem_size_bytes{mountpoint="/"}) * 100)
Panel Configuration:

Title: Disk Usage - Root (%)
Visualization: Stat or Gauge
Unit: Percent (0-100)
Click Apply

Step 2.6: Add Network Traffic Panel
Add a new panel
Add two queries:
Query A (Received):

# TODO: Calculate network receive rate
# Use rate() with node_network_receive_bytes_total
rate(node_network_receive_bytes_total{device!="lo"}[5m])
Query B (Transmitted):

# TODO: Calculate network transmit rate
rate(node_network_transmit_bytes_total{device!="lo"}[5m])
Panel Configuration:

Title: Network Traffic
Visualization: Time series
Unit: bytes/sec
Legend: Show both queries
Click Apply

Step 2.7: Organize Dashboard Layout
Arrange panels by dragging them into a logical layout:

Top row: CPU and Memory gauges
Middle row: Disk usage
Bottom row: Network traffic graph
Resize panels by dragging the bottom-right corner

Click the Save dashboard icon (floppy disk) at the top

Name: System Monitoring Dashboard

Click Save

Step 2.8: Configure Dashboard Settings
Click the gear icon at the top (Dashboard settings)

General Settings:

Add tags: system, monitoring
Set timezone to your local timezone
Time Options:

Set default time range: Last 15 minutes
Auto-refresh: 10s
Click Save dashboard

Step 2.9: Create a Variables (Advanced)
Add a variable to filter by instance:

In Dashboard settings, go to Variables

Click Add variable

Configure:

Name: instance
Type: Query
Data source: Prometheus
Query: label_values(node_cpu_seconds_total, instance)
Multi-value: Enable
Include All option: Enable
Click Apply

Update one of your panels to use the variable:

Edit CPU Usage panel
Modify query to include: {instance=~"$instance"}
Step 2.10: Export Dashboard
Click the share icon at the top
Select Export
Click Save to file
Save as system-dashboard.json
To import later:

Go to Dashboards > Import
Upload the JSON file
Verification Steps
Verify Grafana Installation
# Check Grafana service
sudo systemctl status grafana-server | grep "active (running)"

# Check Grafana is listening
sudo netstat -tlnp | grep 3000
Verify Prometheus and Node Exporter
# Check Prometheus
curl http://localhost:9090/-/healthy

# Check Node Exporter
curl http://localhost:9100/metrics | head -n 5
Verify Dashboard Functionality
Check data is flowing:

All panels should display data
Time series should show activity
No "No Data" messages
Test auto-refresh:

Watch panels update every 10 seconds
Generate load to see changes:
# Generate CPU load
yes > /dev/null &
# Note the PID to kill later
# Kill with: kill <PID>
Test time range selector:

Change time range to "Last 5 minutes"
Verify data updates accordingly
Export verification:

Confirm JSON file was downloaded
Open file and verify it contains panel configurations
Troubleshooting Tips
Grafana Won't Start
# Check logs
sudo journalctl -u grafana-server -n 50

# Verify port is not in use
sudo lsof -i :3000
No Data in Panels
# Verify Prometheus targets
curl http://localhost:9090/api/v1/targets | jq

# Check if metrics are being collected
curl http://localhost:9100/metrics | grep node_cpu
Query Errors
Verify PromQL syntax in Prometheus UI first
Check metric names exist: http://localhost:9090/graph
Ensure time range has data
Dashboard Not Saving
Check browser console for errors (F12)
Verify Grafana has write permissions to its database
Try logging out and back in
